n: 4.1
name: bootstrap-language
kind: hub
is: C is the bootstrap language: one translation unit under about 3,000 lines, a table of C-ABI host doors and a line-count gate. On one M4 Max, MAP_JIT with a per-thread write toggle cost 21 ns a pair and ran unhardened and under the hardened runtime with allow-jit; write-then-mprotect cost 454 ns and was killed under the hardened runtime. Go held a collection 627 ms behind JIT code and paid 23.6 ns per cgo call; Rust reached 50 KB with no_std; Zig is pre-1.0; the old C seed grew to 34,149 lines.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.1
name: jit-host-rules
kind: hub
is: Apple silicon forbids a thread to write and execute one region at once. The documented path is MAP_JIT, a per-thread write toggle and an instruction-cache flush; a hardened-runtime build needs the allow-jit entitlement and gets one MAP_JIT region. Measured on one M4 Max: the toggle path ran in 2 of 3 signing states at 21 ns a pair, and write-then-mprotect was killed under the hardened runtime. The old body's two C carriers already use the MAP_JIT path. Windows and Linux have their own calls, read and not run.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.1.1
name: apple-jit-entitlement-rules
kind: observation
is: Apple's porting page for JIT compilers says Apple silicon enables memory protection for all apps, hardened or not; the allow-jit entitlement is required only with the Hardened Runtime; with both, an app can create only one MAP_JIT region; sys_icache_invalidate must be called before running newly written instructions.
status: relayed
src: 2.11.4
limit: The page carries no publication date.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.1.2
name: apple-allowlist-path-and-ios
kind: observation
is: Apple's porting page says an app with the jit-write-allowlist entitlement can no longer call pthread_jit_write_protect_np and must write through pthread_jit_write_with_callback_np, whose callbacks are declared with the C macro PTHREAD_JIT_WRITE_ALLOW_CALLBACKS_NP, one allowlist per executable. pthread_jit_write_protect_np is not available on iOS.
status: relayed
src: 2.11.4
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.1.3
name: jit-signing-matrix
kind: finding
is: A C test on an M4 Max. With only an ad-hoc signature, MAP_JIT with the toggle and write-then-mprotect both returned 42 and plain RWX mmap failed (Permission denied). Under the hardened runtime, MAP_JIT mmap failed (Invalid argument) without entitlement and worked with allow-jit; the mprotect path was killed by signal 9 in both cases.
status: assumed
limit: One run per cell on one machine under macOS 26.3.1. The kills were seen as signal 9; the kernel's stated reason was not read from the log. Plain RWX was not run under the hardened runtime.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.1.4
name: jit-write-toggle-cost
kind: finding
is: On an M4 Max, one pthread_jit_write_protect_np off-and-on pair cost 20.9 ns; one mprotect read-write to read-execute pair over a 16 KB page cost 453.7 ns, about 22 times more; an indirect call from C into JIT code and back cost 0.79 ns.
status: assumed
limit: One run of a tiny program on one machine; the reading calls its nanosecond figures orders of magnitude, not benchmarks.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.1.5
name: mprotect-publishing-fails-hardened
kind: observation
is: Wasmtime issue 11989, open when read, reports Code Signature Invalid under the hardened runtime for code written read-write and then switched to read-execute; a maintainer confirms that MAP_JIT fixes it. The workaround in use, the allow-unsigned-executable-memory entitlement, carries Apple's warning that it exposes an app to common vulnerabilities.
status: relayed
src: 2.11.6 2.11.5
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.1.6
name: old-body-jit-doors
kind: observation
is: The old body's two C carriers use the MAP_JIT path. map_executable in membrane-boot.c maps RWX with MAP_JIT, lifts pthread_jit_write_protect_np, copies, restores it and calls sys_icache_invalidate. fk_page_alloc in fkwu-uni.c passes the literal flags 0x1802 on Apple; fk_page_write toggles likewise and calls __builtin___clear_cache.
status: observed
src: 2.1.8#558-584 2.1.7#4910-4929
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.1.7
name: old-body-host-reach
kind: observation
is: The old body reaches host libraries through the C ABI and emits its own object file: membrane-boot.c opens the Metal framework with dlopen, fkwu-uni.c binds seven AudioToolbox functions with dlopen and dlsym, and form-macho.fk is a recipe that emits an arm64 Mach-O object which ld links and signs ad hoc.
status: observed
src: 2.1.8#488 2.1.7#4112-4127 2.11.3#1-20
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.1.8
name: other-hosts-executable-memory
kind: observation
is: Windows documents VirtualAlloc, VirtualProtect to PAGE_EXECUTE and FlushInstructionCache for generated code. Linux PR_SET_MDWE (6.3) lets a process irreversibly refuse writable-executable mappings and mappings that turn executable. A V8 blog post of 2023-10-09 calls temporary permission flipping inherently racy and turns to per-thread permissions.
status: relayed
src: 2.11.7 2.11.8 2.11.9
limit: Windows and Linux hosts were read, not run. That a dual mapping of one memory object answers PR_SET_MDWE is from model memory.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.2
name: go
kind: hub
is: Go builds in 0.1 s and calls JIT code at 0.79 ns, and its runtime stays in the process: a collection waited 627 ms for JIT code to return, a cgo call cost 23.6 ns, the pure-Go mprotect path was killed under the hardened runtime, and the Go team does not support JIT frames. Stacks, the internal ABI and signal handling belong to the runtime. wazero and purego show what working around the runtime takes.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.2.3
name: go-gc-waits-for-jit-code
kind: finding
is: With the main goroutine inside a foreign arm64 loop that ran 679 ms, a runtime.GC() requested 50 ms in returned after 627 ms, the rest of the loop. With the same loop compiled by Go (777 ms) the collection returned after 0.31 ms. GOMAXPROCS was 16, Go 1.27.2, M4 Max.
status: assumed
limit: One run of each case.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.2.4
name: go-jit-frames-unsupported
kind: observation
is: Go issue 20123 (2017-04-25) is answered 'We don't support JITed code': frames without stack maps fail the collector's stack scan with 'unexpected return pc'. A golang-dev post of 2026-04-30 cites about 43 ns for a cgo switch against a 2 ns target and proposes a foreign JIT stack-frame protocol. Go 1.26 notes cgo overhead cut by about 30%.
status: relayed
src: 2.11.16 2.11.17 2.11.10
limit: The issue closed the day it opened, and no Go team reply to the 2026 post was visible.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.2.5
name: go-runtime-owns-stacks-abi-signals
kind: observation
is: Go's documents say: goroutine stacks start near 2K and grow or shrink; system stacks cannot grow; the internal ABI changes between versions and on arm64 holds the current goroutine in R28; the runtime installs its signal handlers first and foreign handlers must set SA_ONSTACK; since Go 1.23 the linker refuses linkname to unmarked runtime symbols.
status: relayed
src: 2.11.14 2.11.13 2.11.15 2.11.11
limit: That stack growth relocates the stack is from model memory, not from a fetched page.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.2.6
name: go-precedents
kind: observation
is: wazero, a WebAssembly runtime in pure Go, enters generated code through an assembler trampoline, never lets it call Go, cannot preempt it, and switches code to RX with mprotect. purego calls C without cgo, is beta, and its SyscallN miscalls functions that mix integer and float parameters. Go 1.5 removed its own C by automatic translation into Go.
status: relayed
src: 2.11.18 2.11.19 2.11.20 2.11.21 2.11.12
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.3
name: rust
kind: hub
is: Rust reaches the Apple JIT calls through three extern "C" declarations with no crates and no hidden runtime: 455 KB with std, 50 KB with no_std, 0.09 s rebuilds, a 406 MB toolchain holding the host target only. Naked functions are stable since 1.88. Wasmtime's no_std build omits its compilers, Cranelift and Winch, which need an operating system that makes executable pages.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.3.1
name: rust-jit-measured
kind: finding
is: With rustc 1.99.1 and no crates, MAP_JIT, the write toggle and the icache flush through three extern "C" declarations returned 42. The build took 0.95 s first, 0.09 s after. The binary is 455,384 bytes with std and 50,128 bytes with no_std, no_main, panic=abort and -lSystem; without that link argument the link fails on _mmap.
status: assumed
limit: One machine. The Rust-to-JIT call reading of 1.7 ns differs from C's 0.8 ns because of how the loop was guarded; the reading draws no conclusion from it.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.3.3
name: rust-jit-ecosystem
kind: observation
is: Cranelift calls itself production-ready, with four backends (x86-64, aarch64, s390x, riscv64), APIs not yet stable and a policy of requiring the latest stable Rust. Wasmtime on no_std excludes Cranelift and Winch, so no_std hosts run precompiled code only. dynasm-rs is a proc-macro assembler for x86, x64, AArch64 and RISC-V.
status: relayed
src: 2.11.25 2.11.26 2.11.27
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.4
name: c
kind: hub
is: C meets every duty of the bootstrap without a layer between: the JIT test builds in 0.05 s to 34 KB, Metal answers from about 20 lines at 5.9 ns a message, and Apple clang emits freestanding objects for four foreign targets. LuaJIT, CPython's JIT, MIR, QBE and TinyCC are code generators in C; Zig bootstraps through about 4,000 lines of C; the bootstrappable chain reaches a C compiler from a 256-byte seed. The old seed's 34,149 lines show that C does not stay small unaided.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.4.1
name: c-build-measured
kind: finding
is: With Apple clang 17.0.0 on an M4 Max, cc -O2 built the C JIT test in 0.05 s to a 33,816-byte binary that links only libSystem. Compiling without linking (cc -O2 -c) took 0.19 s for the 1,199-line membrane-boot.c (0.06 s at -O0) and 5.52 s for the 34,149-line fkwu-uni.c.
status: assumed
limit: One run each.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.4.2
name: metal-from-plain-c
kind: finding
is: A plain C program of about 20 lines reaches Metal with no binding layer: dlopen of the Metal framework, MTLCreateSystemDefaultDevice and two objc_msgSend calls print 'Apple M4 Max'. One objc_msgSend property read cost 5.9 ns.
status: assumed
limit: One run on one machine.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.4.4
name: old-seed-size
kind: observation
is: The old body's C seed runtime/fkwu-uni.c is 34,149 lines and its axiom-first carrier runtime/membrane-boot.c is 1,199 lines, counted with wc -l on 2026-11-24. The seed includes <sys/mman.h> when the host has it, declares pthread_jit_write_protect_np by hand, and still passes literal flag values to mmap.
status: observed
src: 2.1.7#1960-1963 2.1.7#4890-4918 2.1.8
limit: The reading says the seed declares mmap by hand; that declaration stands under a guard that is off whenever the header was found, as on this Mac.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.4.5
name: luajit-and-cpython-jits
kind: observation
is: LuaJIT's machine-code allocator, in C, covers POSIX mmap, Windows VirtualAlloc, W^X toggling and a MAP_JIT path with pthread_jit_write_protect_np behind LUAJIT_ENABLE_OSX_HRT. CPython's copy-and-patch JIT (PEP 744) needs LLVM and Clang at build time and nothing at run time; in Python 3.15 it is still experimental.
status: relayed
src: 2.11.28 2.11.29 2.11.30 2.11.31
limit: The fetch tool noted a void function returning a value in LuaJIT's hardened-runtime branch.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.4.7
name: zig-bootstraps-through-c
kind: observation
is: Since 2022-12-07 Zig commits a WebAssembly seed, zig1.wasm.zst of 637 KB compressed, and about 4,000 lines of portable C, a wasm-to-C translator, which together replaced about 80,000 lines of C++. The chain reproduces: zig3 and zig4 match byte for byte.
status: relayed
src: 2.11.36
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.4.8
name: bootstrappable-chain
kind: observation
is: The bootstrappable-builds chain starts from a 256-byte hex0 seed and builds hex1, hex2, M0, a per-architecture cc and M2-Planet, a C subset compiler, for AMD64, x86, AArch64 and RISC-V. Mes builds TinyCC, which builds GCC 2.95, which builds GCC 4.7.4. live-bootstrap automates hex0 to a Linux system.
status: relayed
src: 2.11.37 2.11.38 2.11.39 2.11.40
limit: The Guix full-source-bootstrap post and the GNU Mes home page refused the fetch.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.8
name: keep
kind: hub
is: Carry forward: C as a strict small subset in one translation unit under about 3,000 lines; a table of host doors with C-ABI signatures as the whole public surface; MAP_JIT, a per-thread write toggle and one reserved region from the first line; a line-count gate on the C file; replacement of the C one function at a time until the system writes its own signed image; and the conditions under which the choice of C flips.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.8.1
name: c-as-strict-small-subset
kind: finding
is: The bootstrap is written in a strict C99/C11 subset: one translation unit, a target under about 3,000 lines, no libc use beyond a declared list of OS symbols. The reason is not C's speed: the platform C ABI is the contract that libSystem, objc_msgSend, Vulkan, Win32 and Apple's JIT calls already speak, and a C bootstrap is that contract alone.
status: inferred
conf: high
from: 4.1.1.2 4.1.1.3 4.1.1.6 4.1.1.8 4.1.2.3 4.1.3.1 4.1.4.1 4.1.4.2 4.1.4.7
limit: That the unit also builds with TinyCC and with zig cc is proposed as the test of the subset and was not run. That Vulkan speaks the platform C ABI is assumed; no Vulkan page was read.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.8.2
name: host-door-table
kind: finding
is: The bootstrap's public surface is one table of host doors, entry points with C-ABI signatures: map an executable region, begin and end write for this thread, flush the instruction cache, map a file, load a library, find a symbol, raw call with N words, create a thread, clock, exit. Emitted code receives the table pointer and calls nothing else.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 4.1.1.6 4.1.1.7 4.1.1.8 4.1.4.2
limit: No such table was built or run in the reading.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.8.3
name: jit-contract-from-first-line
kind: finding
is: From its first line the bootstrap reserves one large MAP_JIT region once, writes only inside the per-thread toggle, and flushes the instruction cache after each write. One binary is then correct without the hardened runtime and, given the allow-jit entitlement, with it. A locally built developer binary needs no entitlement and no Apple account.
status: inferred
conf: high
from: 4.1.1.1 4.1.1.3 4.1.1.4 4.1.1.6
limit: Under the hardened runtime without allow-jit the measured MAP_JIT mmap failed, so the reading's phrase 'working in all signing states' overstates its own table. The toggle does not exist on iOS.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.8.4
name: line-count-gate
kind: finding
is: A gate reads the line count of the C file against a hard budget. C gives no memory safety and nothing in the language stops accretion: the old seed reached 34,149 lines. What keeps the bootstrap small is the budget, together with the plan that the C shrinks as emitted code replaces it.
status: inferred
conf: high
from: 4.1.4.4
at: 2026-11-24

n: 4.1.8.5
name: replace-one-function-at-a-time
kind: finding
is: A small evaluator in the bootstrap runs the one grammar's own code generator once. After that, emitted machine code replaces one C-ABI function at a time, until the system writes a signed Mach-O, ELF or PE image that holds its own host doors. The C file then remains as the start from nothing but a C compiler, and the emitted image is the hex seed.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 4.1.1.7 4.1.2.6 4.1.4.5 4.1.4.7 4.1.4.8
limit: Not built.
at: 2026-11-24

